---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { getPost } from '../../data/posts';

const post = getPost('amazon-product-images-best-practices');
---

<ArticleLayout
  kind="blog"
  post={post}
  title="What Is Amazon Product Photography?"
  highlight="Product Photography"
  metaTitle={`${post.title} | Share It Studio Blog`}
>
  <div class="article-body">
    <!-- Cabecera del Artículo -->
    <header class="post-header">
      <p class="intro">
        Amazon product photography is the art of capturing images that communicate to potential customers why they
        should buy your product. This type of photography differs from standard product photos by focusing heavily on
        core features and benefits while maintaining high visual appeal.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Sección: Importancia -->
    <section id="importance">
      <h2>Amazon Images: The Importance of Highlighting Your Product</h2>
      <p>
        When entering the world of online sales, visual content represents your primary point of engagement: the first
        approach, the first interpretation, and most importantly, the foundational step in creating a sales opportunity.
      </p>
      <p>By providing high-quality visual content, customers can easily evaluate your product at a glance.</p>
      <blockquote class="highlight-quote">
        <p>
          <strong>Remember:</strong> Quality of the listing = Quality of the product.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>Your main objective is to convince potential buyers through a brief yet extraordinary visual sample.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Sección: Tipos de imágenes -->
    <section id="image-types">
      <h2>Types of Images on Amazon</h2>
      <p>
        Every Amazon product detail page requires at least one primary image, and Amazon allows you to upload up to 7
        images (including the hero image) along with videos. There are two basic categories every catalog plan should
        include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Hero Images:</strong> The core photo that represents a product in search results and browse pages,
          serving as the primary image on the product detail page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Secondary Images:</strong> Includes infographics and lifestyle images designed to elaborate on product
          utility.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="image-classification-example">
        <h3>Visual Breakdown Example</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>a) Hero image:</strong> Represents the product in search and detail pages.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>b) Infographics:</strong> Visual representations of information or data through graphics, helping
            shoppers digest features quickly and clearly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>c) Lifestyle image:</strong> Designed to show your product in action, helping buyers envision what
            using the product is like.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>
        According to Content Marketing Statistics, people following directions with text and illustrations perform 323%
        better than people following directions without illustrations. Taking advantage of this is essential!
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Sección: Requisitos -->
    <section id="requirements">
      <h2>Amazon Product Images Requirements</h2>
      <p>To ensure compliance and optimal performance, your images must meet strict Amazon standards:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Images must accurately represent the product for sale.</li>
        <li>Images must closely match the product title.</li>
        <li>
          The product must fill at least <strong>85%</strong> of the image frame.
        </li>
        <li>
          The optimal zoom experience requires files to be <strong>1600px or larger</strong> on the longest side
          (minimum 1000px for zoom, 500px minimum for standard site display).
        </li>
        <li>
          Supported formats include JPEG (.jpg/.jpeg), TIFF (.tif), PNG (.png), or GIF (.gif). PNG is preferred.
          Animated GIFs are not supported.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Prohibited Elements (Must Not):</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Exceed 10,000px on the longest side.</li>
        <li>Be blurry, pixelated, or feature jagged edges.</li>
        <li>
          Contain nudity or be sexually suggestive. Kids, baby leotards, underwear, and swimwear must not be shown on
          human models.
        </li>
        <li>
          Include Amazon logos, trademarks, or confusingly similar variations (e.g., AMAZON, PRIME, ALEXA, Amazon
          Smile).
        </li>
        <li>
          Include Amazon badges or similar variations (e.g., 'Amazon’s Choice', 'Premium Choice', 'Best Seller'). Refer
          to official trademark guidelines for details.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Sección: Estándares de Hero Images -->
    <section id="hero-standards">
      <h2>Site Standards for Hero Images</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Must feature a pure white background (RGB color values of 255, 255, 255) to blend seamlessly with Amazon
          pages.
        </li>
        <li>
          Must show the entire product for sale without touching or being cut off by the edge of the frame (except for
          certain jewellery like necklaces).
        </li>
        <li>
          Must show products outside their packaging (boxes, bags, or cases should not appear unless they are an
          essential product feature).
        </li>
        <li>Must show a single shoe facing left at a 45-degree angle.</li>
        <li>
          Must show the product on a human model or lying flat on a surface (invisible mannequins are allowed as long as
          the product is not obscured).
        </li>
        <li>
          Must be professional photographs of the actual product (graphics, illustrations, mock-ups, and placeholders
          are prohibited, and excluded accessories or props must not confuse customers).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Your Hero Images Must Not:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Show a human model sitting, kneeling, leaning, or lying down (models must be standing).</li>
        <li>
          Show any part of a mannequin for clothing accessories, regardless of mannequin type (clear, solid-colour,
          flesh-toned, framework, or hanger).
        </li>
        <li>
          Show kids' and baby clothing on a human model; they must be shown lying flat or using an invisible mannequin.
        </li>
        <li>Include multiple views of a single product.</li>
        <li>Include text, logos, borders, color blocks, watermarks, or graphics over the product or background.</li>
      </ul>

      <aside class="pro-tip">
        <p>
          <strong>Tip:</strong> If you want to highlight your brand logo or additional attributes, use infographics. If
          your product naturally includes the logo, make sure it is clearly visible in your hero images to enhance brand
          recognition.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- Sección: Best Practices -->
    <section id="best-practices">
      <h2>Amazon Product Images Best Practices</h2>

      <h3>For Hero Images</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Show the product clearly:</strong> The more visual information you provide, the better. Shoppers skim
          through thousands of listings and rely heavily on hero images to choose the right fit quickly. For example, if
          a listing shows a phone alongside a charger and earpods while another shows only the phone, the extra visual
          context can drive higher conversions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consider using hand models or props:</strong> If your product involves touch or interaction, hand
          models or props help communicate its utility. For instance, including a female hand model on a touch light
          listing targeted at mothers looking to enhance baby sleep can drastically improve engagement and sales
          conversion. Always appeal to your customer persona's pain points.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>For Infographics</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep it to no more than 4 features:</strong> Infographics are designed to reassure shoppers. Avoid
          overcrowding; stick to a maximum of 4 essential features while keeping them detailed enough to prove utility.
          Be clear, concise, and focused.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Combine with lifestyle images:</strong> Merging text features with real-life lifestyle images
          significantly improves comprehensiveness and conversion rates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Optimize text size:</strong> Ensure text is perfectly readable—not excessively large to waste space,
          nor too small requiring shoppers to zoom out.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>For Lifestyle Images</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Focus on product interaction:</strong> Models should actively interact with and look at the product
          rather than staring blankly at the camera, keeping the product as the true protagonist of the image.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Add a short description (4 words max):</strong> Brief copy overlaid on lifestyle images helps transmit
          your message and address customer pain points effectively.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
